' The ONE definition of the standard dialog result shape.
'
' Every dialog in the JRDialog family resolves by writing this assoc array to its
' own per-instance `result` field. Callers read the same keys no matter which
' dialog ran, which is what lets a call site swap presentation (button row <->
' scrollable list, as showChoiceDialog does) without touching its handler.
'
' These builders exist so the shape has exactly one producer. Hand-building the
' assoc array in each component is how the three copies drifted apart before
' (JRListDialog hardcoding `confirmed: false`, JRKeyboardDialog hardcoding
' `buttonIndex: -1`). The mapping from "what the user did" to
' cancelled/confirmed is the part every call site depends on, so it belongs in
' pure functions that can be unit-tested without a scene.
'
' Keys:
'   cancelled   - user backed out (back / Cancel); no selection was made
'   confirmed   - user chose the affirmative action
'   buttonIndex - index into `buttons`, or -1 when the dialog has no button row
'   optionIndex - index of the chosen option, or -1 when nothing was chosen
'   buttonText  - label of the chosen button ("" when none)
'   value       - entered text (keyboard dialogs only; "" otherwise)
'   checkedIndexes - the ticked rows of a checklist, ascending ([] from every other dialog)
'   externallyCancelled - the CANCEL came from code, not from the user
'
' externallyCancelled is the one key that answers "who closed this?" rather than
' "what did the user choose?", and it exists for handlers that ACT on their
' result. `cancelled` cannot tell the user pressing Back from a third party
' clearing the screen, on purpose: that is what stops an owner being stranded
' with no result at all. But an owner whose handler navigates or mutates state
' needs the difference, or someone else's dialog fires its action. It is true
' only on the overlay family's cancelDialog() path (cancelOpenDialog and
' presentOverlayDialog's supersede) and false for every user-driven resolution,
' cancels included.
'
' ⚠️ The KEYBOARD dialog cannot report it, and it reports FALSE rather than
' nothing: a positive claim that the USER closed it. It resolves through Roku's
' modal `close` field, which reads identically whether the user backed out or
' code wrote it. presentOverlayDialog never supersedes that channel, but
' cancelOpenDialog DOES reach it (`scene.dialog.close = true`), so the wrong
' answer is reachable rather than theoretical. Safe today only because neither
' keyboard caller ACTS: SetServerScreen.onServerUrlEntered and
' ConfigList.onConfigValueEntered both apply their value on `confirmed`. Trust
' this key on the overlay family only, and do not build an acting handler on a
' modal dialog.
import "pkg:/source/utils/misc.bs"

' Result for a button-row dialog (JRDialog). index = -1 means cancelled.
' confirmIndex is the button that counts as the affirmative action (-1 = none),
' which is what `showConfirmDialog` sets so callers can read result.confirmed.
function buttonDialogResult(index as integer, confirmIndex as integer, buttons as object, externallyCancelled = false as boolean) as object
  buttonText = ""
  if index >= 0 and isValid(buttons) and index < buttons.count()
    buttonText = toString(buttons[index])
  end if

  ' optionIndex mirrors buttonIndex: for choice dialogs the buttons ARE the
  ' options, so showChoiceDialog callers read result.optionIndex on both routes.
  return makeDialogResult(index < 0, index >= 0 and index = confirmIndex, index, index, buttonText, "", externallyCancelled)
end function

' Result for a selection-list dialog (JRListDialog). index = -1 means dismissed
' without choosing. A list dialog has NO buttons at all: its rows are the only
' focusable thing in it and Back is the exit, so both button fields stay empty
' and callers branch on optionIndex / cancelled.
'
' It used to take a `cancelText` and report it as buttonText on dismissal, from
' when the dialog had a Cancel button. Keeping that would have meant a result
' naming a control the user was never shown.
function listDialogResult(index as integer, externallyCancelled = false as boolean) as object
  return makeDialogResult(index < 0, false, -1, index, "", "", externallyCancelled)
end function

' Result for a text-entry dialog (JRKeyboardDialog). `cancelled` distinguishes a
' cancel/back from an intentionally empty entry, so callers can tell them apart.
function textDialogResult(cancelled as boolean, enteredText as string) as object
  value = ""
  if not cancelled then value = enteredText
  ' externallyCancelled is left false: see the ⚠️ note at the top of this file.
  return makeDialogResult(cancelled, not cancelled, -1, -1, "", value, false)
end function

' Result for a checklist (JRListDialog with `multiSelect`). A tick counts when it is
' made and Back only leaves, so there is no confirm step to report: the result says
' what the set is now and whether it changed.
'
'   cancelled  - nothing changed, or code closed the dialog. A handler that reloads
'                on a change reads this and does nothing.
'   confirmed  - the set changed.
'   checkedIndexes - the set at close. A close from code reports the set the dialog
'                opened with: the viewer did not choose to leave, so their ticks are
'                not taken as an answer.
'
' @param {object} initial - the ticked indexes the dialog opened with
' @param {object} current - the ticked indexes now
' @param {boolean} externallyCancelled - true when code closed the dialog
function checklistDialogResult(initial as object, current as object, externallyCancelled = false as boolean) as object
  opened = normalizeCheckedIndexes(initial)
  if externallyCancelled then return makeDialogResult(true, false, -1, -1, "", "", true, opened)

  final = normalizeCheckedIndexes(current)
  changed = not sameCheckedIndexes(opened, final)
  return makeDialogResult(not changed, changed, -1, -1, "", "", false, final)
end function

' The set after OK on one row: the row leaves the set if it was in it, and joins it
' if it was not. Returns a new array, ascending.
function toggleCheckedIndex(checked as object, index as integer) as object
  toggled = []
  found = false
  for each entry in normalizeCheckedIndexes(checked)
    if entry = index
      found = true
    else
      toggled.push(entry)
    end if
  end for
  if not found then toggled.push(index)
  return normalizeCheckedIndexes(toggled)
end function

' A set of ticked row indexes in its one form: integers, ascending, each once.
' Anything that is not a whole number at or above 0 is dropped, and so is an index
' at or past `itemCount` when one is given.
'
' @param {dynamic} indexes - what a caller passed; invalid reads as none
' @param {integer} itemCount - the number of rows, or -1 to skip the upper bound
function normalizeCheckedIndexes(indexes as dynamic, itemCount = -1 as integer) as object
  normalized = []
  if type(indexes) <> "roArray" then return normalized

  seen = {}
  for each entry in indexes
    entryType = LCase(type(entry))
    if entryType = "roint" or entryType = "rointeger" or entryType = "integer"
      index = int(entry)
      key = index.toStr()
      if index >= 0 and (itemCount < 0 or index < itemCount) and not seen.doesExist(key)
        seen[key] = true
        normalized.push(index)
      end if
    end if
  end for

  normalized.sort()
  return normalized
end function

' Whether two normalized sets hold the same indexes.
function sameCheckedIndexes(a as object, b as object) as boolean
  if a.count() <> b.count() then return false
  for i = 0 to a.count() - 1
    if a[i] <> b[i] then return false
  end for
  return true
end function

function makeDialogResult(cancelled as boolean, confirmed as boolean, buttonIndex as integer, optionIndex as integer, buttonText as string, value as string, externallyCancelled = false as boolean, checkedIndexes = invalid as object) as object
  if not isValid(checkedIndexes) then checkedIndexes = []
  return {
    cancelled: cancelled,
    confirmed: confirmed,
    buttonIndex: buttonIndex,
    optionIndex: optionIndex,
    buttonText: buttonText,
    value: value,
    externallyCancelled: externallyCancelled,
    checkedIndexes: checkedIndexes
  }
end function
